## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: 8b217592ce18bd392a54064140001b6ceef89bd4

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** The fix commit `52fa0d5` touches only `ZNode.java`, reverting a single line in the `ZNode` constructor from `ParentNode.SetChild(this, ZNode._EnforcedParent);` back to `this.ParentNode = ParentNode;`. I traced the file's history with `git log --follow -p` and found the exact commit that made the opposite change (introducing the buggy line).

**(2) The introducing change:** Commit `8b21759` (same author, ~30 minutes before the fix, same day) changed:
```java
this.ParentNode = ParentNode;
```
to:
```java
ParentNode.SetChild(this, ZNode._EnforcedParent);
```

**(3) Evidence:** Looking at `SetChild`'s implementation:
```java
public final ZNode SetChild(ZNode Node, boolean EnforcedParent) {
    assert(this != Node);
    if(EnforcedParent || Node.ParentNode == null) {
        Node.ParentNode = this;
    }
    return Node;
}
```
This is an instance method invoked *on* `ParentNode`. If a `ZNode` is constructed as a root node (i.e., `ParentNode == null`, a legitimate/common case, e.g. top-level AST nodes), the buggy constructor code calls `null.SetChild(...)`, causing a `NullPointerException`. The original/reverted code (`this.ParentNode = ParentNode;`) handles `ParentNode == null` safely by simply assigning null to the field.

The fix commit is a pure one-line revert of `8b21759`'s change, with no other logic altered, strongly indicating `8b21759` is the sole bug-introducing commit.

I'm confident in this identification — the fix is a literal textual revert of that specific line from that specific commit, and the semantic difference (null-safety of field assignment vs. NPE-prone method call on a potentially-null receiver) clearly explains the bug.

